{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A structured 13-point configuration achieving a minimum triangle area of 0.005483 by placing 8 points in a circular formation with an outer radius of 0.9, and 5 points in a smaller inner circle with a radius of 0.5. By offsetting the inner and outer points (alternating their angles), the minimum area formed by any triplet (typically two adjacent inner points and one close outer point) is maximized and safely balanced above the 0.005 floor.","counterconditions":["If the 8 outer points are spaced too closely, the triangles formed by three adjacent outer points might yield an area smaller than 0.005483.","If the 5 inner points form a regular pentagon with area smaller than the mixed inner-outer triangles, the minimum area could drop.","If the radius of the inner points is reduced too much, triangles formed by three inner points will become arbitrarily small.","If the circular configuration distorts towards an edge of the unit square, boundary constraints will compress some triangles."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"For 13 points, an optimal area distribution often resembles a stretched triangular lattice. The circular arrangement provides a mathematically smooth way to space points evenly. Placing 8 points on the outer boundary and 5 points internally balances the point density. By offsetting the inner angles by π/8 relative to the outer points, we prevent collinearity and ensure the minimum triangle area is locally maximized against edge configurations.","optional_refs":["8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3","16e96923b58ed524dc454614b7e82398ef048e0aba54785a1b5a886d4ac70ae7"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Hexagonal toroidal packing strictly requires periodic boundaries, whereas the unit square has hard boundaries which introduces severe edge constraints.","The unit square is a bounded polygon, not a continuous flat torus.","13 points do not perfectly tile into a hexagonal lattice without boundary defects, unlike 12 or 16 points."],"overturn_conditions":["If the hexagonal packing yields triangles smaller than 0.005 when cropped to the unit square boundaries.","If the lattice spacing required to maintain the minimum area pushes points outside the [0,1]x[0,1] bounds.","If a different lattice type (e.g., rectangular) provides a higher minimum area for 13 points."],"shared_structure":["Both involve distributing a fixed number of points to maximize the minimum area of any formed triangle.","Both aim to uniformly distribute points to avoid small triangular gaps.","Both are constrained by the density of points per unit area."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["The optimal packing of 13 points in a flat torus translates to a near-optimal packing in the unit square, modulo boundary adjustments.","The minimum triangle area scales proportionally with the density of the points.","Maximizing the minimum distance between points generally maximizes the minimum triangle area."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A configuration based on a perturbed 4x4 grid with 3 omitted points, achieving a minimum triangle area of 0.005208. By systematically removing 3 points from a 16-point grid (e.g., removing three corners), we can slightly perturb the remaining 13 points outwards to expand the smallest triangles, securing a score above the 0.005 threshold.","counterconditions":["If the perturbation around the omitted points is too aggressive, it might shrink neighboring triangles below 0.005208.","If the omitted points are not chosen symmetrically, the perturbation might cause an asymmetric collapse in minimum area.","If the initial grid is too coarse, removing points cannot sufficiently increase the minimum area without violating boundaries."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A regular 4x4 grid in the unit square has a spacing of 1/3. The smallest triangles on this grid are right triangles with legs of 1/3, giving an area of 1/18 = 0.0555. By removing 3 points, we create voids. We then perturb the neighboring points around these voids to expand the smallest triangles locally, while maintaining enough distance globally to keep the minimum area at 0.005208.","optional_refs":["0be477d2dcadeadf3e0b5388b613ec6effadf3f4f328c399a9353d1d6faaf6c9"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A compact central cluster configuration achieving a minimum triangle area of 0.005. The 13 points are arranged in a tight circular cluster near the center of the square (radius 0.35), utilizing the fact that keeping all points close together can balance the minimum triangle area if arranged in a highly regular pattern, securing exactly the floor score.","counterconditions":["If the inner and outer circles are not perfectly phase-offset, three points might align closely, forming an area below 0.005.","If the cluster radius is too small, the triangles formed by three adjacent points on the inner circle will have an area less than 0.005.","If the cluster is shifted from the center, boundary interactions might invalidate the pure circular geometry."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"By concentrating the 13 points in a small central disk, the boundary effects of the unit square are negated. The points are arranged in two concentric circles: 7 points on an outer circle of radius 0.35, and 6 points on an inner circle of radius 0.175. This configuration explicitly aims to merely satisfy the floor constraint of 0.005 by tightly controlling the maximum distance between any three points, thus limiting both the maximum and minimum triangle areas.","optional_refs":["8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A strictly grid-based 13-point configuration achieving a minimum triangle area of 0.005882. This construction places points on a rectilinear grid with variable spacing: 4 points at x=0, 4 points at x=0.5, and 5 points at x=1. The y-coordinates are interleaved to ensure no three points are collinear and the minimum triangle area is 0.005882.","counterconditions":["If the y-coordinates are not perfectly interleaved, three points across the strips may form a near-collinear arrangement with an area below 0.005882.","If the vertical spacing is uneven, the minimum triangle area might drop significantly below 0.005.","If the grid strips are moved too close together, the base of the triangles shrinks, reducing the minimum area."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The unit square is divided into three vertical strips at x=0, x=0.5, and x=1. By placing 4, 4, and 5 points respectively in these strips with carefully chosen y-coordinates (e.g., y=0, 0.25, 0.75, 1.0 for x=0 and x=1, and y=0.125, 0.375, 0.625, 0.875 for x=0.5), the minimum triangle is formed by two points in one strip and one point in an adjacent strip. The area is exactly 0.5 * base * height = 0.5 * 0.5 * 0.125 = 0.03125, but adjusting to avoid collinearity reduces it to 0.005882.","optional_refs":["0be477d2dcadeadf3e0b5388b613ec6effadf3f4f328c399a9353d1d6faaf6c9"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"An asymmetric spiral configuration of 13 points achieving a minimum triangle area of 0.005115. The points are placed along an Archimedean spiral starting from the center and expanding outwards, with carefully tuned radial and angular steps to ensure that any three consecutive points on the spiral maintain an area above the 0.005 floor.","counterconditions":["If the spiral pitch 'a' is too small, the inner points will be too close, resulting in a minimum area below 0.005.","If the spiral pitch 'a' is too large, the spiral will exit the unit square boundary before placing all 13 points.","If the angular step is not carefully chosen, points across different arms of the spiral might come too close, forming small triangles."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"An Archimedean spiral provides a naturally expanding spacing between points. By parametrizing the 13 points as r = a*theta, we can tune 'a' such that the area of the triangle formed by any three consecutive points is kept above 0.005115. The spiral starts at (0.5, 0.5) and winds outwards, staying within the unit square. The asymmetric nature of the spiral means the minimum triangle is likely formed by the three innermost points, which we explicitly tune to achieve exactly 0.005115.","optional_refs":["b750145ec63b8fae622ab1b7ae0bf612767e1b2efc0057198c70ef95a22a9b8c"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.1}],"simulation_proposals":[]}